3.45.332.874 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"In the context of operating systems and concurrent programming, **processes** and **threads** are fundamental units of execution, but they differ significantly in terms of resource usage, communication, and performance. Here's a breakdown of their differences and when to choose one over the other in a **web server** context.\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 **Process**\nA **process** is an independent instance of a running program. Each process has:\n\n- Its own **memory space** (code, data, heap, stack).\n- Its own **system resources** (file descriptors, environment variables, etc.).\n- A **process ID (PID)**.\n- **Isolation** from other processes — one process crashing doesn’t directly affect others.\n\n**Inter-process communication (IPC)** is required for processes to share data (e.g., pipes, sockets, shared memory), which is **slower and more complex** than thread communication.\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 **Thread**\nA **thread** is the smallest unit of execution within a process. All threads within a process share:\n\n- The **same memory space** (global variables, heap).\n- The **same file descriptors and resources**.\n"}
